# Line-ending policy.
#
# `text=auto` normalizes to LF in the index for anything git detects as text, and leaves
# everything else alone. The previous line was `* text eol=autocrlf`. Its `text` half worked --
# which is why the index is already 100% LF -- but the `eol` half was inert: `eol` accepts only
# `lf` or `crlf`, so git stored the literal string, could not act on it, and the WORKING-TREE
# ending fell back to the per-machine `core.autocrlf`. The explicit rules below pin that per file
# type, so a contributor with a different `core.autocrlf`, or a container build, gets the same
# bytes. `text=auto` is used rather than a bare `* text` so a future binary file is not
# force-converted.
* text=auto

*.ps1    text eol=lf
*.psd1   text eol=lf
*.psm1   text eol=lf
*.ps1xml text eol=lf
*.md     text eol=lf
*.json   text eol=lf
*.yml    text eol=lf
*.yaml   text eol=lf
*.txt    text eol=lf
*.sh     text eol=lf
*.svg    text eol=lf
*.mof    text eol=crlf

# Ensure binary files are never line-ending converted. `text=auto` already auto-detects, but
# these are declared so a future addition cannot be misdetected and mangled.
*.exe   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.xl*   binary
*.pfx   binary
*.png   binary
*.dll   binary
*.so    binary
*.cer   binary
*.ico   binary
*.zip   binary
*.nupkg binary
